# Target C-II — COLUMN-UNIQUE SIGNED braids share one fixed `B_0` fence cell

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the SIGNED / COMMON-CUT / COLUMN-UNIQUE branch; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the 15-spaced one-sign strong-TB1 Horn upper family through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** SIGNED common-cut robust-owner-grid theorem; proper-crosscut train pseudosegment model; pairwise noncrossing of the common `B_0` train family; line/train uniqueness  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_signed_column_unique_fence_cell_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The previous SIGNED common-cut theorem leaves one exact alternative in which

\[
D^{\varepsilon_K-o(1)}
\]

pairwise-crossing inactive near-diameters meet every train of the fixed linear family `B_0` at most once. The `B_0` trains are pairwise noncrossing proper crosscuts and have one common order on every robust owner row.

That uniqueness converts the whole branch into an interval system.

Number the trains

\[
b_1,b_2,\ldots,b_M,
\qquad
M>0.0783D-o(D),
\]

in their common crosscut order. They divide the source disk into `M+1` fence cells. For a retained SIGNED chord `e=xy`, let `c(x),c(y)` be the two endpoint-cell ranks.

Because `e` meets each `b_j` at most once and all such meetings are proper, it meets exactly the consecutive set

\[
\boxed{
I_B(e)
=\{\min(c(x),c(y))+1,\ldots,\max(c(x),c(y))\}.
}
\]

Equivalently the chord is confined to the closed fence interval

\[
J_B(e)
=[\min(c(x),c(y)),\max(c(x),c(y))].
\]

Any two retained SIGNED chords cross properly. Their closed fence intervals therefore cannot be disjoint: a `B_0` crosscut lying strictly between two disjoint intervals would separate the two straight segments. Hence the intervals `J_B(e)` are pairwise intersecting. One-dimensional Helly gives one fixed cell rank `g_D` lying in every interval.

Thus every chord of the polynomial SIGNED braid connects a cell on the left of `g_D` to a cell on the right of `g_D`, allowing equality. After one four-way pigeonhole, a polynomial sub-braid has one of four exact forms:

1. **THROUGH:** every chord passes strictly through the common cell and therefore crosses the two bounding `B_0` trains once each;
2. **LEFT-ANCHORED:** every chord has one endpoint in the common cell and the other on the left;
3. **RIGHT-ANCHORED:** every chord has one endpoint in the common cell and the other on the right;
4. **CELL-INTERNAL:** both endpoints of every chord lie in the same fixed `B_0` cell.

The surviving size is still

\[
D^{\varepsilon_K-o(1)}.
\]

So COLUMN-UNIQUE no longer permits a polynomial crossing braid spread among unrelated train-fence cells. The entire obstruction is forced through one fixed cell of the same source-wide `B_0` fence.

---

## 1. The fixed `B_0` family is an ordered noncrossing fence

Use the COLUMN-UNIQUE outcome of the preceding robust-owner-grid theorem. It supplies:

- a pairwise-properly-crossing vertex-disjoint SIGNED chord family `E_D` with
  \[
  |E_D|=D^{\varepsilon_K-o(1)};
  \]
- a fixed global transverse train family `B_0` with
  \[
  |B_0|>0.0783D-o(D);
  \]
- at least `D^(epsilon_K-o(1))` robust owner rows, each crossed by every `b in B_0`;
- and, crucially,
  \[
  \boxed{
  e\text{ meets }b\text{ at most once}
  \quad(e\in E_D,\ b\in B_0).
  }
  \tag{FC.1}
  \]

The common-grid theorem gives that the trains in `B_0` are internally noncrossing and occur in one common order on every robust owner row. In the proper-crosscut model they therefore form an ordered fence

\[
b_1<\cdots<b_M,
\qquad M=|B_0|.
\tag{FC.2}

The fence has cells

\[
C_0,C_1,\ldots,C_M,
\tag{FC.3}

where `C_s` is the component between `b_s` and `b_{s+1}` with the usual exterior conventions at `s=0,M`.

Every source point not on a train belongs to exactly one such cell. If an endpoint lies on a train, perturb the cell convention infinitesimally in the inherited orientation; the blocker endpoints are source vertices whereas train curves run through contact-edge midpoints, so in the present midpoint model this degeneracy does not occur.

---

## 2. COLUMN-UNIQUE gives an exact consecutive incidence interval

Take a chord

\[
e=xy\in E_D
\]

and write

\[
p=c(x),\qquad q=c(y),
\qquad 0\le p,q\le M.
\tag{FC.4}

Assume `p<q`; the other orientation is symmetric.

Every train

\[
b_{p+1},b_{p+2},\ldots,b_q
\]

separates the endpoint cells `C_p` and `C_q`, so the connected segment `xy` must meet it at least once. By (FC.1) it meets it exactly once.

Every train `b_j` with `j<=p` or `j>q` has both endpoints on the same side. Any intersection between the straight line and the train is proper because the line direction lies a fixed positive angular distance outside that train's midpoint-step cone. A same-side arc has an even number of proper intersections with a separating proper crosscut; (FC.1) allows at most one. Hence there are none.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
I_B(e)=\{p+1,p+2,\ldots,q\}.
}
\tag{FC.5}

Define the closed endpoint-cell interval

\[
\boxed{
J_B(e)=[p,q].
}
\tag{FC.6}

Equation (FC.5) also says that the whole segment `e` is contained in the closed union of fence cells and boundary trains indexed by `J_B(e)`. It cannot leave that fence slab without crossing one of its boundary trains, which would add a forbidden extra train intersection.

---

## 3. Pairwise crossing forces pairwise interval overlap

Take two distinct retained chords `e,f`. They cross properly by construction.

Suppose their closed cell intervals were disjoint. After relabeling,

\[
\max J_B(e)<\min J_B(f).
\tag{FC.7}

Choose any train `b_k` whose index separates those two cell intervals. By (FC.5), neither chord crosses `b_k`: both endpoints of `e` lie on one side and both endpoints of `f` on the other.

Since `b_k` is a proper crosscut, the two closed fence slabs containing `e` and `f` are disjoint. The two straight segments therefore cannot meet, contradicting their proper crossing.

Hence

\[
\boxed{
J_B(e)\cap J_B(f)\ne\varnothing
\quad\text{for every }e,f\in E_D.
}
\tag{FC.8}

The family consists of closed intervals on a line. Pairwise intersection is therefore equivalent to total intersection:

\[
\boxed{
\bigcap_{e\in E_D}J_B(e)\ne\varnothing.
}
\tag{FC.9}

Choose one integer cell rank

\[
g_D\in\bigcap_{e\in E_D}J_B(e).
\tag{FC.10}

This rank depends on the source but not on the blocker.

Thus every retained SIGNED chord has endpoint-cell ranks `p,q` satisfying

\[
\boxed{
\min(p,q)\le g_D\le\max(p,q).
}
\tag{FC.11}

---

## 4. Four exact common-cell normal forms

Orient each chord by its fence-cell ranks so that

\[
p\le q.
\]

Because `g_D in [p,q]`, exactly one of the following holds:

### THROUGH

\[
p<g_D<q.
\tag{FC.12}
\]

If `0<g_D<M`, then (FC.5) shows that every THROUGH chord crosses both trains bounding the common cell,

\[
b_{g_D},\qquad b_{g_D+1},
\]

exactly once. At an exterior cell only the existing boundary train is present.

### LEFT-ANCHORED

\[
p<q=g_D.
\tag{FC.13}
\]

One endpoint lies in `C_{g_D}` and the other lies strictly to its left.

### RIGHT-ANCHORED

\[
p=g_D<q.
\tag{FC.14}
\]

One endpoint lies in `C_{g_D}` and the other lies strictly to its right.

### CELL-INTERNAL

\[
p=q=g_D.
\tag{FC.15}
\]

Both endpoints lie in the same fixed fence cell `C_{g_D}` and the chord avoids every `B_0` train.

These four classes partition `E_D`. Retain the largest. The resulting sub-braid `E_D^{cell}` satisfies

\[
\boxed{
|E_D^{cell}|\ge{1\over4}|E_D|
=D^{\varepsilon_K-o(1)}.
}
\tag{FC.16}

Every chord in `E_D^{cell}` remains:

- vertex-disjoint from every other retained chord;
- pairwise properly crossing;
- an inactive pair of length `D-O(D^-1)`;
- SIGNED with the same fixed macroscopic negative `w_*` projection;
- supported by the same robust-owner construction from the preceding theorem.

Only its exact fence-cell relation has been purified.

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The SIGNED / COMMON-CUT branch now has exact normal forms on both sides of the row/column uniqueness fork.

```text
ROW-UNIQUE:
    Theta(log D) one-sided crossing cable;
    D^(epsilon_K/2-o(1)) robust co-phased rows;
    one fixed >0.0783D column family B_0.

COLUMN-UNIQUE:
    D^(epsilon_K-o(1)) pairwise-crossing SIGNED chords;
    each is a pseudosegment relative to every b in B_0;
    all closed B_0 endpoint-cell intervals share one fixed cell C_g;
    after a four-way split all chords have one exact THROUGH / LEFT-ANCHORED /
    RIGHT-ANCHORED / CELL-INTERNAL relation to C_g.
```

The next COLUMN-UNIQUE target is metric. THROUGH gives a polynomial braid crossing one fixed adjacent pair of `B_0` trains exactly once each. The anchored cases put one endpoint population in one fixed train cell. CELL-INTERNAL traps both endpoint populations of every near-diameter in that single fixed cell. Any bound on the metric width or source capacity of one `B_0` cell would therefore attack the whole remaining COLUMN-UNIQUE branch at once.

No such cell-width bound is claimed here.

**Disposition:** converted COLUMN-UNIQUE into one fixed train-fence-cell obstruction and reduced it to four exact cell types. C-II and Level I remain open.